Yes. You can run the same Hadoop WordCount program on your laptop. Since you've already got Hadoop working in your terminal, you don't need to change the Java program.

1. Go to your project
cd ~/073

2. Check your files
ls


You should have:

WordCount.java
wordcount.jar
input.txt

3. Start Hadoop
start-dfs.sh
start-yarn.sh


Check:

jps


You should see:

NameNode
DataNode
SecondaryNameNode
ResourceManager
NodeManager
Jps

4. Create HDFS input
hdfs dfs -mkdir -p /home/hadoop/input


Put your local file into HDFS:

hdfs dfs -put -f input.txt /home/hadoop/input/


Check:

hdfs dfs -ls /home/hadoop/input

5. Remove old output
hdfs dfs -rm -r -f /home/hadoop/output

6. Run WordCount on your laptop
hadoop jar wordcount.jar WordCount /home/hadoop/input /home/hadoop/output


Wait until you see:

map 100% reduce 100%
Job ... completed successfully

7. See the result
hdfs dfs -cat /home/hadoop/output/part-r-00000


For:

hello hadoop hadoop mapreduce


you'll get approximately:

hadoop  2
hello   1
mapreduce  1

If you mean "run it without Hadoop"

If by "on my laptop" you mean you want to run the Java program directly without Hadoop/HDFS/YARN, that's different. Your current WordCount.java is specifically a Hadoop MapReduce program, so it requires Hadoop.

If you're doing this for your college practical, the commands you've just used are exactly the Hadoop-on-laptop workflow.